    "text": "To determine the length of the shorter part of the rope, we start by understanding the given ratio and the total length of the rope. The ratio of the two parts is 2:3, and the total length of the rope is 40 meters.\n\nFirst, we need to find the sum of the parts of the ratio. The ratio 2:3 means that the total parts are \\(2 + 3 = 5\\).\n\nNext, we calculate the length of each part by dividing the total length of the rope by the sum of the parts of the ratio. This gives us:\n\\[\n\\text{Length of each part} = \\frac{40 \\text{ meters}}{5} = 8 \\text{ meters}\n\\]\n\nSince the shorter part corresponds to the smaller part of the ratio, which is 2 parts, we multiply the length of one part by 2:\n\\[\n\\text{Length of the shorter part} = 8 \\text{ meters} \\times 2 = 16 \\text{ meters}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the shorter part of the rope is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).",
